40 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049737 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428441 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049737 #edb/ref 1031 "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049737 #edb/ref 1033 1906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049737 #edb/ref 1034 0.9141522462766187 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049737 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050453 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050453 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050453 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050453 #edb/ref 1031 "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050453 #edb/ref 1033 1907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050453 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1031 "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1033 3029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1034 0.2853007808149399 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "508ac00e-0867-53e8-a192-d981ff4bbdc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186418998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186418998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183193 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "90535267-04a3-5209-88fc-9a5564318779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184065 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184065 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cb88713-f830-501e-9b0a-60320936ad14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184065 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the axioms of a vector space, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom [math]{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}[/math]}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom [math]{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}[/math]}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184094 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184094 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V$ be the set of all real polynomials of degree exactly $3$, equipped with the usual addition and scalar multiplication operations. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$V$ is **not** a vector space because $\\textrm{_________}\\,.$*\n\nI. the sum of two polynomials of degree $3$ may not be a polynomial of degree $3$\n\nII. $V$ does not contain the polynomial $-p(x)$ for every $p(x) \\in V$\n\nIII. addition of polynomials is not commutative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "132efc65-5f50-5582-8955-a5d34c57af04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184094 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer  is as follows:\n\n*$V$ is **not** a vector space because **the sum of two polynomials of degree $3$ may not be a polynomial of degree $3$.** *\n\nFor instance, if $p(x) = 1 +x^3$ and $q(x) = 1-x^3$, then $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are in $V.$ However  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x)+q(x) & =(1+{x}^{3})+(1-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\notin V.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative.\n\n- For any polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $3$, the corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ also has degree $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1039 "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184167 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184196 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1045 "Defining Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186184930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186184420 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c358bde0-c269-53bb-bce4-8d51f4516e17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184761 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184761 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184761 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184761 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $W$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree $5$ or greater. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184761 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac2c9af3-5c5a-5bc3-898a-7e78bc1b3b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184761 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **$W$ does not contain the zero element.** *\n\nIndeed, the zero polynomial has degree $0$, which is less than $5.$\n\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- For any polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $5$ (or greater), the corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ has the same degree. $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184930 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184930 #edb/ref 1061 653101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184930 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "708bbadc-ca98-5148-98dd-f1cc4b9125d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184930 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184931 #edb/ref 1061 653106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0564ce26-4a8b-562e-9ab6-4940e223854b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184969 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184969 #edb/ref 1061 653103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184969 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca7e22db-059b-547f-ad06-b3fe2c740ff4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184969 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185014 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185014 #edb/ref 1061 653102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185014 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b2df015-d32e-5a46-ab00-33e8ccdd4686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185014 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185317 #edb/ref 1064 "In previous lessons, we've seen how some sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication operations exhibit properties similar to $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nIn this lesson, we will formally define what we mean by a **vector space.**\n\nFirst, let $V$ be a set equipped with the following two operations:\n\nVector addition: \n\nFor any two elements $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V,$ there is a unique element $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under vector addition.**\n\nScalar multiplication: \n\nFor any element $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ and any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R},$ there is a unique element $\\alpha \\mathbf{x}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under scalar multiplication.**\n\nA set $V$ equipped with vector addition and scalar multiplication forms a **vector space** if it satisfies the following **vector space axioms**. \n\nAxioms of Vector Addition ($+$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A1]}$: $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y} =  \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$; vector addition is **commutative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A2]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z} =  \\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}, \\mathbf{z} \\in V$; vectors addition is **associative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A3]}$: There exists a **zero-vector** $\\mathbf{0}$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{x}$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A4]}$: For every $\\mathbf{x} \\in V,$ there exists $-\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + (-\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0}$; $-\\mathbf{x}$ is called the **negative** of $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nAxioms of Scalar Multiplication ($\\cdot$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A5]}$: $\\color{blue}1 \\cdot \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$; multiplying by $1$ does not change a vector.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A6]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\nAxioms Connecting Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication (the Distributive Laws)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A7]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{x} = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\beta \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A8]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{y}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\alpha \\mathbf{y}$ for any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$.\n\nSo, a vector space is simply a collective term for all kinds of sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication, where these operations satisfy this particular list of axioms (which are similar to the properties of these operations in $\\mathbb{R}^n$). \n\nThe elements of any vector space are called **vectors**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185317 #edb/ref 1065 3187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185317 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "488db0ee-92f8-5790-8ff6-2977ab0e6dce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185317 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185345 #edb/ref 1064 "There are many different vector spaces. Some of the most important are listed below.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^n$, the set of all column vectors of height $n$ with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_n(\\mathbb{R})$, the set of all $n \\times n$ matrices with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, the set of all polynomials in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$, the set of all polynomials of degree less than or equal to $n$ in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The set of all real-valued functions of one variable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185345 #edb/ref 1065 6199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185345 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eb7bd53e-69ec-5e14-b970-1bd6ff9aff7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185345 #edb/ref 1067 "Important Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242269 #edb/ref 1080 "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in F$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242269 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242269 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c2c12e0-1cd2-57dc-bb2e-fccfbfe29b6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242706 #edb/ref 1080 "addition and scalar multiplication of functions in $F$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242706 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242706 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aed90e2c-7d1b-5ac3-9ce4-8c895e1d0385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242959 #edb/ref 1080 "addition of $f,g\\in F$ is not commutative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242959 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242959 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c9bc4983-8538-5113-9d78-363014648f95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242990 #edb/ref 1080 "addition of $f,g\\in F$ is not associative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cdb145c6-2092-56d4-9ff1-6bbe59e69ca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243041 #edb/ref 1080 "$F$ does not contain the zero element" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243041 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243041 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d3235266-f3fb-58a4-8cd7-6dda3548ef28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186243041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243548 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "39664c8c-a6d4-545a-b91d-30b8ab061a3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243958 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$F$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe3a1a04-348a-505c-9183-5288567e7407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243958 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$F$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **$F$ does not contain the zero element.** *\n\nIndeed, evaluating the zero function $f(x)=0$ at $x=0,$ we have $f(0)=0\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in F$ is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in F$ is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in F.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of functions satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321524 #edb/ref 1080 "$W$ does not contain the zero element" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321524 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321524 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30f17da9-a2bd-5534-adad-c7eb18896578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321654 #edb/ref 1080 "addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials do not satisfy the distributive laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321654 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321654 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40484eb7-546a-59c5-8075-7334001f71a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321919 #edb/ref 1080 "addition of polynomials is not commutative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "63232a3a-2f7e-5f2a-8880-e7b2f5f26a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322079 #edb/ref 1080 "addition of polynomials is not associative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322079 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322079 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "77129de6-3842-5621-aee0-6e8854935738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322887 #edb/ref 1080 "$W$ does not contain the polynomial $-p(x)$ for every $p(x)\\in W$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322887 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322887 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dec5bb24-cf51-550f-854c-ba44585b5dc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186321524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323648 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f229d374-9057-5731-87e8-a3f0484852d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342683 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342683 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342683 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "360cc03a-3f39-5879-9a88-a61073b7fcf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343177 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A5}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343177 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343177 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d6880fc-5f2b-5192-95d6-4d52a478dfb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343222 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343222 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343222 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71d6a532-cd4b-5d34-ae27-3901b1065d79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344014 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A5}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344014 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344014 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ca10db7b-bf57-5c5b-a421-62ab3bb870b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344289 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A8}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344289 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344289 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ea3d0561-a8a8-5898-828f-77cac082232d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186342683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344730 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "86bd575b-d1b5-591b-a355-2d1fcad9bf66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344968 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $x$ is a vector such that $-x+w=w$ for some $w\\in V$, then $x=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ x+(-x+w) & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{adding }x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (x+(-x))+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{adding }-w\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+(w+(-w)) &  & [\\,\\text{axioms  A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4dc6297-c39f-58c6-a8af-023dc6a275e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344968 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ x+(-x+w) & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{adding }x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (x+(-x))+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{adding }-w\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+(w+(-w)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382854 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $u$ and $v$ are vectors such that $u+x=v+x$ for some $x\\in V,$ then $u=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+x & =v+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e00386b5-a859-5a68-9df5-ac8710a4ede8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382854 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+x & =v+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382855 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382855 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218638